--- /dev/null
+++ b/.toolbox/STANDARD.md
@@ -0,0 +1,102 @@
+# S1: no direct ORM queries on person tables
+
+**The standard.** From `AGENTS.md`: "Never add new direct ORM queries like `Person.objects.filter(...)`
+or `PersonDistinctId.objects.filter(...)`; use the existing routed helpers or create new ones
+following the established pattern."
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Violation | Each occurrence of `Person.objects`, `PersonDistinctId.objects`, `CohortPeople.objects`, `Group.objects` or `GroupTypeMapping.objects` in a `.py` file (regex `\b(Person\|PersonDistinctId\|CohortPeople\|Group\|GroupTypeMapping)\.objects\b`) |
+| Exempt: tests | Any file under a `test/` or `tests/` directory, `test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, `conftest.py` |
+| Exempt: routed helpers | `posthog/models/person/util.py` (the routed person helpers), `posthog/models/group_type_mapping.py` (a routed helper), `posthog/models/person/person.py` (the model module; its managers back the routed helpers) |
+| Fix | Use (or add) a routed helper in `posthog/models/person/util.py` |
+| Enforcement | A ratchet: the counts per file recorded in `.toolbox/results.json` may only go down. A new file must arrive with none. CI: `.github/workflows/ci-toolbox-standards.yml` |
+| Campaign | `person-orm-zero`: matches and files with matches, from the recorded start (102 matches in 37 files, 2026-11-21) to zero. See [Campaign history](#campaign-history) |
+
+It is scored by [Toolbox](https://github.com/loganvolkers/toolbox) (`toolbox report`), from
+`.toolbox/toolbox.yaml`, which includes a vendored copy of Toolbox's `pattern-count` package
+(`.toolbox/packages/pattern-count`, v0.1.0). Install `toolbox` and `toolbox-connector-workspace` (0.1.1)
+on your `PATH`, plus `jq`.
+
+## Commands (run from the repository root)
+
+**Report**: every violation, by file, as JSON (`[{"file": ..., "count": ...}]`):
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox -o failures | jq '[.[] | select(.scorecard == "person-orm") | {file: .path, count: .measure}] | sort_by(.file)'
+```
+
+For the line numbers too, drop the `jq` (each failure's `evidence` is `line N`, one per match).
+
+**Gate**: exit 1 when a file has more violations than recorded, or a new file has any; exit 0
+otherwise. Files that went down are listed as `✓ better`, followed by "out of date: run with
+--update", which means the baseline can be tightened:
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox --baseline .toolbox/results.json -o failures --out /dev/null
+```
+
+`✗ worse` lines name each regression (`evidence grew 2 → 3`, or `new, at none`). `· changed ... new
+(clean)` lines are new Python files without violations: harmless, recorded on the next `--update`.
+
+**Campaign**: progress toward zero (matches and files with matches, baseline vs now, percent done):
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox --baseline .toolbox/results.json -o json 2>/dev/null | jq '.campaigns["person-orm-zero"].sinceStart | {baseline: .measure.from, current: .measure.now, percentDone: .measure.percentDone, files: {baseline: .entitiesShort.from, current: .entitiesShort.now, percentDone: .entitiesShort.percentDone}}'
+```
+
+The gate also prints it as one line: `campaign person-orm-zero since <date>: ... measure 102 → 101 (1.0% of the way)`.
+The baseline is the count recorded when the campaign was first recorded, not today's baseline: it
+does not move when you tighten.
+
+## After fixing violations: tighten the baseline
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox --baseline .toolbox/results.json --update -o failures --out /dev/null
+git add .toolbox/results.json && git commit -m "chore(toolbox): tighten S1 baseline"
+```
+
+`--update` refuses to write while anything regressed. Never hand-merge `.toolbox/results.json`: on a
+conflict, take the target branch's copy and rerun `--update`.
+
+## Changing the standard
+
+All of it lives in the `with:` block of `.toolbox/toolbox.yaml`.
+
+- **Add a model** (for example `GroupPerson`): add it to `pattern`, e.g.
+  `'\b(Person|PersonDistinctId|CohortPeople|Group|GroupTypeMapping|GroupPerson)\.objects\b'`. Then record its existing
+  violations as intended, not as regressions, and commit both files:
+
+  ```bash
+  toolbox report .toolbox --baseline .toolbox/results.json --update --accept person-orm -o failures --out /dev/null
+  ```
+
+  `--accept person-orm` lists the newly recorded violations; review them. The campaign restarts from
+  the new state.
+- **Exempt a routed helper**: add `path: reason` under `exempt`, then run the `--update` command
+  above (plain `--update` is enough: an exemption only removes violations). When the same change also
+  adds a model, run the `--accept` command once for both.
+- **Exempt a whole kind of file** (for example migrations): add a glob under `exclude`, e.g.
+  `"**/migrations/**"`, then `--update`. In globs `**` spans directories (including none, so
+  `"**/test_*.py"` also matches a root-level `test_x.py`) and `*` does not.
+- After any change: `toolbox validate .toolbox`, then check the Report output.
+
+## Campaign history
+
+The campaign's starting point is recorded in `.toolbox/results.json` (`campaigns.person-orm-zero`).
+
+| Date | Change | Start (matches / files) | Why |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 2026-11-21 | Standard first enforced (`Person`, `PersonDistinctId`, `CohortPeople`) | 80 / 28 | First recording |
+| 2026-11-21 | Added `Group` and `GroupTypeMapping`; exempted `posthog/models/person/person.py` | 102 / 37 | Restarted on purpose (`--update --accept person-orm`) |
+
+Why the restart: the scope changed in both directions. The new models add matches in 10 new files
+and 4 files that already had some; they are the campaign's new work. The exemption removes
+`person.py`'s matches, which were never fixed, only ruled out. Keeping the old start (80 / 28) would
+have shown the campaign going backwards (-27.5%) for work that was just added, and would have
+counted the exemption as progress. No progress was lost: the old campaign was still at 0% (80 → 80)
+when it was restarted. Restart again only for a deliberate change to the standard, and add a row
+here.
+
+A textual pattern can be gamed (an alias such as `P = Person; P.objects`); reviewers still apply the
+standard's intent.
